<title>CANADA: Alberta to end hog export monopoly - Paszkowski.</title>
<headline>Alberta to end hog export monopoly - Paszkowski.</headline>
<dateline>EDMONTON 1996-11-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Alberta's provincial government on Wednesday said it plans to end the Alberta Pork Producers Development Corporation's hog export monopoly by December 2, 1996.</p>
<p>&quot;The regulations will take effect December 2, 1996,&quot; Alberta Agriculture Minister Walter Paszkowski said in a statement.  </p>
<p>A vote will occur within two years of the implementation of open marketing to &quot;ensure the new system is the right one for both producers and packers,&quot; Paszkowski said.</p>
<p>Manitoba was the first Canadian province to eliminate its hog export monopoly July 1, 1996. Saskatchewan is investigating whether it should end its monopoly.</p>
<p>Canada exported 341.05 million tonnes of pork in calendar 1995 valued at C$967.55 million, Agriculture Canada statistics show.</p>
<p>Alberta produced three million hogs last year, Manitoba produced 2.5 million and Saskatchewan, 1.0 million.</p>
<p>-- Gilbert Le Gras 204 947 3548</p>
